# Development settings: `cp .env.example .env`. Host processes (`make serve`, `make agent-worker`), docker compose and the Makefile all read it.
# Production's counterpart is deploy/prod/prod.env.example. A name in both means the same thing, and each example lists every variable its compose files read
# (tests/core/test_env_examples_match_compose.py). A commented line shows the default used while it stays commented.
#
#   Part 1  Running the app    LLM, data stores, identity and the front door, channels, tools, observability
#   Part 2  Tuning             retrieval, workers, spend and time ceilings, retention, billing
#   Part 3  Developer tooling  evaluation judges (promptfoo, deepeval) and security scanners

# =====================================================================
# Part 1: running the app
# =====================================================================

# --- LLM access (everything goes through the LiteLLM proxy, which speaks the OpenAI API) ---
OPENAI_API_BASE=http://localhost:4000/v1
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-anything          # what a HOST process (`make serve`, `make agent-worker`) sends to LiteLLM: the master key unless you mint a scoped one
# The containerized app (`make up-app`) does not read OPENAI_API_KEY: compose hands THIS variable to the api and workers as theirs (unset: the dev master key).
# Mint a scoped, budget-capped key with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=... make litellm-key ARGS="create --max-budget <usd> --budget-duration 1d"`, then set both names to it.
# LITELLM_APP_KEY=

CHAT_MODEL=chat                     # a model *alias* from deploy/dev/litellm-config.yaml
EMBED_MODEL=embed                   # the embedding alias, same file
# Strict-mode tool schemas (every argument required, optional ones nullable). Leave false on local Ollama: it ignores them and the smallest model calls tools worse (pattern 74).
# Turn on only with a provider that enforces strict function calling, and run `make probe-tools` before and after.
# TOOL_CALLS_STRICT=false
# `chat-medium` and `chat-strong` are Gemini models in dev: the LiteLLM container reads GOOGLE_API_KEY (below, "Google AI Studio key"; README, "Model tiers").
# nomic-embed-text's card says its input "must include" a task prefix (`search_query: `, `search_document: `; quote the value to keep the trailing space). The app has never sent them.
# TURNING ONE ON CHANGES THE EMBEDDING SPACE: rebuild every collection first (`make reembed COLLECTION_NAME=...`) or retrieval is refused as an identity mismatch.
# Decide with `make model-eval`, not by assumption (specs/014).
# EMBED_QUERY_PREFIX=
# EMBED_DOCUMENT_PREFIX=
# Bump when you re-pull the embedding model under the same name (an Ollama tag moved): the alias resolver cannot see it.
# EMBED_MODEL_REVISION=
# EMBED_IDENTITY_CACHE_SECONDS=10.0   # how long a collection's identity check is cached
# MODEL_RESOLVE_TTL_SECONDS=60.0      # how long the gateway's "which model is behind this alias" is trusted; a re-pointed `embed` alias is noticed within this

# --- Model routing (specs/014, 015; pattern 68) ---
# Choose a model tier per turn from rules over the message plus a topic label from the ML service, and call that tier's gateway alias. OFF and inert by default.
# `shadow` makes and counts every decision (agent_model_route_tier_total{mode="shadow"}) but runs the turn on CHAT_MODEL: read what routing WOULD have spent first. `on` acts.
# shadow/on refuse to start unless MEDIUM_CHAT_MODEL or STRONG_CHAT_MODEL names an alias different from CHAT_MODEL and from each other; an empty tier does not exist and a
# decision for it steps down. Dev's `chat-medium` and `chat-strong` are free-tier Gemini models (GOOGLE_API_KEY): sample data only, and small quotas.
# MEDIUM_CHAT_MODEL=
# STRONG_CHAT_MODEL=
# ROUTER_MODE=off                # off | shadow | on
# ROUTER_MAX_TIER=strong         # the ceiling: no turn is routed above it (local | medium | strong); one operator-wide value
# ROUTER_LABELS=true             # ask the ML service for the message's topic (POST /v1/decide); false = rules only
# ROUTER_LONG_PROMPT_CHARS=1500  # the long_prompt rule fires at this length (>= 200)
# ROUTER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1.5     # the label call's deadline; a miss leaves the rules to decide. Keep it above twice ML_CLASSIFY_DEADLINE_MS

# --- Postgres (two databases in the one the compose stack runs) ---
# The checkpointer holds graph state AND paused approvals, so a restart loses neither (pattern 16); appdata holds the usage ledger, tickets, CRM, incidents and tool-call idempotency keys.
# The defaults are the compose stack's own credentials: set real ones for anything else.
# CHECKPOINTER_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://langfuse:langfuse@localhost:5432/checkpointer
# APPDATA_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://langfuse:langfuse@localhost:5432/appdata
# CHECKPOINTER_POOL_MAX_SIZE=10  # raising it alone does not raise checkpoint throughput past one connection per process; add worker replicas
# APPDATA_POOL_MAX_SIZE=10       # app/platform/sql_store.py's pool, per process
# The containers go through PgBouncer (deploy/dev/infra.yml, specs/022: at most 18 server connections per database however many replicas run). A host process uses Postgres
# directly by default; to use the pooler too, point it at port 6432: postgresql://langfuse:langfuse@localhost:6432/appdata (and /checkpointer).

# --- Redis (the queue, locks and front-door state; also the answer cache unless it has its own) ---
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# REDIS_MAX_CONNECTIONS=300  # every queued SSE connection holds one for the whole turn

# --- Qdrant ---
QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6333
COLLECTION=docs

# --- Object storage for uploads (the compose stack's `minio`) ---
# The keys are MinIO's own local defaults: set real ones for anything else.
# MINIO_ENDPOINT=localhost:9000
# MINIO_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
# MINIO_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin
# MINIO_BUCKET=ingest-uploads
# MINIO_SECURE=false  # plain http for local compose; true for a real deployment

# --- Who is calling the API (app/adapters/inbound/api/deps.py::get_ctx; specs/018) ---
# `identity` (the default): the API trusts only the signed identity the front door (authlimit) adds to a request it verified, ignores the client's X-Tenant-Id / X-Principal-Id,
# and refuses to start without AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_URL. `headers` trusts those two headers: right for running the API alone on your machine (`make serve`, which is why this file sets
# it), dangerous for anything a stranger can reach, since any client can claim any tenant.
AUTH_MODE=headers
# Where the front door publishes its public signing keys (identity mode only), e.g. http://authlimit:8080/internal/jwks.json. Plain http is accepted for a loopback address or an
# in-network name without a dot; anything else must be https.
# AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_URL=
# Seconds a fetched key set is trusted (a rotated-in key is fetched on the next unknown `kid` anyway), and how long the last set stays trusted when the front door cannot be reached (0 = not at all).
# AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_CACHE_SECONDS=60
# AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_STALE_GRACE_SECONDS=600
# The built-in web page's sign-in (identity mode only). It must match what the front door was started with: its issuer, its audience (RFC 8707) and the scope it requires. Defaults: the local stack's.
# WEB_AUTH_ISSUER=https://auth.localhost:8443
# WEB_AUTH_AUDIENCE=https://api.localhost:8443
# WEB_AUTH_SCOPE=api:chat
# The containerized web client (`make up-app`): the loopback port it is published on. Behind the front door the page is at https://api.localhost:8443/ instead.
# WEB_PORT=8085

# --- Clients of the chat API: the CLI and Telegram (specs/020) ---
# They call the API through the front door and sign in at WEB_AUTH_* above. CHANNEL_API_URL: http://localhost:8000 reaches the API directly (what AUTH_MODE=headers is for),
# https://api.localhost:8443 the local stack's door. CHANNEL_CA_FILE is the PEM of the CA that signed the door's certificate when it is not in the system store (`make front-door-ca`
# exports Caddy's local root); naming it REPLACES the system roots. The CLI signs in with a browser and a one-shot listener on CHANNEL_CLI_CALLBACK_PORT (registered by
# `make front-door-bootstrap`; change both together) for CHANNEL_CLI_TENANT (blank = DEFAULT_TENANT), and keeps its session in CHANNEL_CLI_SESSION_FILE
# (blank = ~/.config/graph-agent-core/channel-session.json, mode 600).
# CHANNEL_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
# CHANNEL_CA_FILE=
# CHANNEL_CLI_CALLBACK_PORT=33420
# CHANNEL_CLI_TENANT=
# CHANNEL_CLI_SESSION_FILE=

# --- The front door's own settings (deploy/dev/gateway.yml and app.yml; `make front-door-up`) ---
# Read by docker compose, not by the Python app, and only for the `gateway` profile. Each default suits the local stack (*.localhost names, Caddy's own CA, port 8443), so none needs
# setting. Production's counterparts are in deploy/prod/prod.env.example.
# The issuer authlimit accepts (Casdoor's `origin`) and the chat API's public address, the audience of every token it checks.
# AUTHLIMIT_ISSUER=https://auth.localhost:8443
# AUTHLIMIT_AUDIENCE=https://api.localhost:8443
# `enforce` refuses an over-limit request with a 429; `observe` serves it and counts what it would have refused (for sizing a new limit).
# AUTHLIMIT_LIMIT_MODE=enforce
# Seconds the Casdoor signing keys are cached (the minimum between two refreshes is 1-3600 and never above the cache time) and how long the last set stays trusted when Casdoor
# cannot be reached. `make authlimit-smoke` shortens them to watch an outage in seconds.
# AUTHLIMIT_JWKS_CACHE_SECONDS=300
# AUTHLIMIT_JWKS_MIN_REFRESH_SECONDS=30
# AUTHLIMIT_JWKS_STALE_GRACE_SECONDS=21600
# How often (100-60000 ms) each authlimit replica reads the revocation list.
# AUTHLIMIT_REVOCATION_POLL_MS=1000
# Accept signing-key files readable by group or others, for a development bind mount that cannot be chmod 600. Never in production.
# AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_ALLOW_LOOSE_PERMS=false
# Where the two gateway containers push OTLP metrics and where the MCP gateway's ops tools read Prometheus: the ports `make obs-up` and `make collectors-up` publish on the host.
# An empty value turns an OTLP push off.
# AUTHLIMIT_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:4318
# MCP_GATEWAY_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://host.docker.internal:4318
# MCP_GATEWAY_PROMETHEUS_URL=http://host.docker.internal:9090
# Not set here: CASDOOR_DB_PASSWORD and CASDOOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD are generated once by `make front-door-up` into ~/.cache/graph-agent-core-front-door/secrets.env (a second
# --env-file outside the repo); the Makefile sets AUTHLIMIT_UID, AUTHLIMIT_GID and AUTHLIMIT_KEYS_DIR (the signing keys: `make authlimit-keys`).

# --- MCP servers this app PUBLISHES (app/adapters/inbound/mcp/server.py, ops_server.py; `make mcp-serve`, `make mcp-serve-ops`) ---
# Who the server acts for. Set by whoever LAUNCHES it, never passed as a tool argument: the connected assistant fills the arguments in, so a tenant there is a tenant a prompt
# injection can name (pattern 21). A desktop client puts the same two names in the `env` block of its server entry (README, "MCP servers (stdio)"). The server refuses to start
# without them; the operations server needs only the principal (its data is not tenant-scoped).
# MCP_TENANT=
# MCP_PRINCIPAL=

# --- The MCP gateway (app/adapters/inbound/mcp/gateway.py; specs/017; `make front-door-up`) ---
# The published servers over Streamable HTTP behind OAuth 2.1: the identity comes from a verified access token on every request (the claims below), not from MCP_TENANT/MCP_PRINCIPAL.
# A resource server only; the tokens come from the authorization server named here. Empty issuer or JWKS URL = the gateway refuses to start.
# The origin clients use (no path). Each server's audience is this + its path (/ecorp/mcp, /ops/mcp); also the only Host/Origin answered.
# MCP_GATEWAY_PUBLIC_URL=https://mcp.localhost:8443
# The issuer exactly as a token's `iss` has it (e.g. https://auth.localhost:8443) and where to fetch its signing keys (e.g. http://casdoor:8000/.well-known/jwks: an in-network name
# inside Docker, which is why they are two settings).
# MCP_GATEWAY_ISSUER=
# MCP_GATEWAY_JWKS_URL=
# Which claims name the tenant and the principal (Casdoor: `owner` is the organization, `sub` a stable user id).
# MCP_GATEWAY_TENANT_CLAIM=owner
# MCP_GATEWAY_PRINCIPAL_CLAIM=sub
# Tenants allowed on the operations server (comma-separated). Its data is the deployment's, not a tenant's; empty = nobody.
# MCP_GATEWAY_OPS_TENANTS=
# Seconds the signing keys are cached (a rotated key is fetched on the next unknown `kid` anyway).
# MCP_GATEWAY_JWKS_CACHE_SECONDS=300

# --- API protection and uploads ---
# How fast a tenant or a person may start turns is not an app setting: the front door enforces it (services/authlimit, spec 018).
# CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=*        # fine for the local demo; narrow it for a real deployment (deploy/prod/prod.env.example)
# MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE_MB=25         # per file, enforced before any object-store write
# MAX_UPLOAD_FILES_PER_REQUEST=5
# CHAT_MESSAGE_MAX_CHARS=32000  # the longest message POST /chat/stream/queued takes (a 422 naming the field above it); about 8,000 tokens
# CHAT_MAX_IMAGES=4             # most images on one chat request; the web page attaches one
# CHAT_IMAGE_MAX_CHARS=7000000  # the longest one image (a data URI or a URL) may be, in characters: about 5 MB of picture

# --- Telegram (app/adapters/inbound/channels/telegram/bot.py, `make telegram`) ---
# Optional: only `make telegram` needs a token (a bot from @BotFather), and it refuses to start without one (pattern 42).
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# Each Telegram user is a person `telegram-<id>` of ONE tenant at the authorization server (specs/020); the bot signs in as them with a password DERIVED from this secret
# (`openssl rand -hex 32`, at least 32 characters, never changed afterwards: a new value is a new password for every Telegram person). Required with AUTH_MODE=identity.
TELEGRAM_PERSON_SECRET=
# The tenant (Casdoor organization) they belong to; blank = DEFAULT_TENANT.
# TELEGRAM_TENANT=
# An operator adds people (`make telegram-person ACTION=add TELEGRAM_ID=<id>`); an unknown user is told so. AUTO_PROVISION creates them on a first message instead, with the client
# id and secret of the tenant's `api-<tenant>` application. MEASURED: those are an administrator's of the WHOLE organization (they can reset anyone's password), so turning it on
# puts a tenant-wide credential in the bot's environment. Leave it off, or give the bot a tenant of its own.
# TELEGRAM_AUTO_PROVISION=false
# TELEGRAM_PROVISION_CLIENT_ID=
# TELEGRAM_PROVISION_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The domain (app/domains/registry.py) the CLI and the bot talk to: ecorp, support, ops or sales (README, "Example domains"). `make telegram-support` / `telegram-sales` set it.
AGENT_DOMAIN=ecorp
# After this many auto-declined approval pauses on ONE turn, an unattended caller (Telegram with TELEGRAM_APPROVALS=false) is told the request needs a person's approval and the run
# is cancelled (app/agent/runtime/stream.py::astream_events_turn_unattended, pattern 8).
# UNATTENDED_MAX_DECLINE_ROUNDS=3

# --- Telegram: transport, approvals, replies, account linking (specs/021) ---
# `poll` (default) long-polls Telegram and needs no public address. `webhook` needs a public HTTPS URL ending in /telegram/webhook that your proxy forwards to the bot's listener, and a
# secret Telegram sends back on every request (32-256 of A-Z a-z 0-9 _ -; `openssl rand -hex 32`). The proxy must NOT ask for the front door's token on /telegram/*: Telegram has none,
# the secret is the check.
# TELEGRAM_MODE=poll
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_URL=   # e.g. https://bot.example.com/telegram/webhook
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The bot's own listener (webhook and the linking pages). Loopback by default; TLS is the proxy's job. The local stack's Caddy routes https://api.localhost:8443/telegram/* to
# host.docker.internal:8081 (deploy/shared/caddy/gateway.caddyfile), charged per address, not by token: for that to reach a bot started with `make telegram`, set the listen host to
# 0.0.0.0 (which opens it to your network). Production runs the bot as a service behind the same routes (deploy/README.md).
# TELEGRAM_LISTEN_HOST=127.0.0.1
# TELEGRAM_LISTEN_PORT=8081
# TELEGRAM_MAX_CONCURRENT_CHATS=8   # chats answered at once; one chat's messages stay in order
# A turn needing approval shows Approve / Reject buttons: only its owner can decide, and an unanswered one expires. false: the worker declines every approval, so nothing mutating
# is done from Telegram.
# TELEGRAM_APPROVALS=true
# TELEGRAM_APPROVAL_TTL_SECONDS=900
# A reply grows in one message as it is written (edited at most once per interval) instead of arriving at the end.
# TELEGRAM_STREAM_REPLIES=true
# TELEGRAM_STREAM_EDIT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2.0
# Account linking: /login sends the person to the authorization server's own page and Telegram then acts as THE ACCOUNT THEY SIGN IN AS (TELEGRAM_PERSON_SECRET is not used). Needs AUTH_MODE=identity,
# the bot's public HTTPS address (callback <it>/telegram/link/callback, registered with `make front-door-bootstrap ARGS="--telegram-link-url <it>"`) and a Fernet key that seals the
# refresh token kept per link:
#   python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
# Losing the key unlinks everyone (they send /login again); leaking it, with Redis, exposes the refresh tokens.
# TELEGRAM_LINKING=false
# TELEGRAM_LINK_PUBLIC_URL=   # e.g. https://bot.example.com
# TELEGRAM_LINK_KEY=
# TELEGRAM_LINK_STATE_TTL_SECONDS=600
# The bot as a container (`make telegram-up`, profile `telegram`): the same bot, in the shape production runs it, behind the front door. It reads the TELEGRAM_* settings above and its
# domain from TELEGRAM_AGENT_DOMAIN (the host bot's is AGENT_DOMAIN); `make telegram-up` itself sets TELEGRAM_UPSTREAM (where Caddy sends /telegram/*) and TELEGRAM_CA_FILE (Caddy's root certificate).
# TELEGRAM_AGENT_DOMAIN=ecorp

# --- Ops bot (app/domains/ops/, `make ops-digest`) ---
# The Prometheus of the observability stack (`make obs-up`): the ops bot's fetch_metrics_summary tool and scripts/jobs/ops_digest.py query it directly.
PROMETHEUS_URL=http://localhost:9090

# --- Team-channel notifications (app/adapters/outbound/notify/team_channel.py) ---
# Used by the ops bot's post_to_team_channel and the support and sales domains' escalate_to_human / handoff_to_human. Unset: a local var/team_channel.log sink; set: also a
# best-effort POST to this Slack incoming webhook.
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- crawl4ai server (app/adapters/outbound/crawl4ai/web_crawler.py; deploy/dev/tools.yml; pattern 50) ---
# Required for the container to be reachable: since crawl4ai 0.9.0 it binds loopback inside its container without a token, and the published port resets. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
CRAWL4AI_API_TOKEN=
# Where the container is reachable from this process (11235 is its real listen port).
# CRAWL4AI_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:11235

# --- Web search (app/adapters/outbound/web/serper.py, app/domains/shared/web_search.py; pattern 87) ---
# Key from https://serper.dev/api-keys. Empty: `web_search` is declared but answers that search is not set up (no network call). Each search spends one credit and sends its query to
# Serper and Google, so the tool is `outward`: every call waits for a human approval. `make web-search-check` spends one credit to confirm the key works.
SERPER_API_KEY=
# WEB_SEARCH_MAX_RESULTS=5   # results one search hands the model (1-10; also what Serper is asked for; 10 is its default page)

# --- OpenSandbox server (app/adapters/outbound/mcp/sandbox_tools.py; deploy/dev/tools.yml; `make sandbox-up`; pattern 50) ---
# Required: opensandbox-server refuses to start non-interactively without an api_key. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
OPENSANDBOX_API_KEY=
# host:port it listens on. 8090, not its packaged 8080: open-webui binds 8080 here, and a request landing on it looked like a real 405.
# OPENSANDBOX_MCP_DOMAIN=localhost:8090
# Image and lifetime of the one sandbox each conversation lazily creates and reuses. Build the image with `make sandbox-build`; bump both together for more packages or longer
# pauses between approvals.
# SANDBOX_IMAGE=graph-agent-core-sandbox:latest
# SANDBOX_TTL_SECONDS=1800

# --- Bounds on what a tool hands the model (app/core/output_limits.py; specs/009 A3) ---
# The most text one remote MCP tool call or sandbox result may return: head and tail are kept, the middle becomes a marker saying how much. 20,000 is the page reader's own cap.
# MCP_TOOL_OUTPUT_MAX_CHARS=20000
# The longest command, script, path or file content the model may send a sandbox tool (a validation error names the limit).
# SANDBOX_ARG_MAX_CHARS=20000

# --- Langfuse tracing (keys come from the Langfuse UI after `make up`) ---
LANGFUSE_HOST=http://localhost:3000
LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=
LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=
# Seconds process exit waits for the tracing client to flush (a down Langfuse otherwise stalls exit ~90 s).
# LANGFUSE_SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5

# --- Metrics and profiles (app/platform/telemetry.py, profiling.py) ---
# Where the API and workers push OTel metrics over OTLP/HTTP: the optional collector (`make collectors-up`, published on this port). Unreachable is fine: a failed push is dropped.
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4318
# Distributed tracing goes to the same collector (RUNTIME_PATTERNS.md 113): the share of requests whose spans are exported, 0 to 1. Log lines carry `trace_id` either way.
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLE_RATIO=1.0
# Continuous profiling (specs/022): where to push CPU profiles. Blank is off and the SDK is never imported. For flame graphs from a host process run `make obs-up` and set
# http://localhost:4040 (the observability stack's Pyroscope); the containers in deploy/dev/app.yml point at it already.
PYROSCOPE_SERVER_ADDRESS=
# PYROSCOPE_SAMPLE_RATE=100   # samples per second: lower it to trade resolution for overhead

# =====================================================================
# Part 2: tuning
# Every line shows its default; uncomment a commented one to change it. Each is a field of app/core/config.py's Settings, whose comments carry the full
# reasoning. tests/core/test_env_example_matches_settings.py fails if a setting is missing here.
# =====================================================================

# --- Retrieval (pattern 20) ---
# ML_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8083  # the ml-service container: the cross-encoder reranker and the prompt-injection classifier
# SPARSE_MODEL=Qdrant/bm25              # local BM25 sparse-vector model (fastembed)
# HYBRID_PREFETCH_LIMIT=20              # candidates pulled per leg (dense, sparse) before fusion
# RERANK_TOP_K=5                        # results kept after the rerank

# --- The ML service's own environment (services/ml-service/ml_service/settings.py): set on the ml-service container, not in the app ---
# ML_MODELS_ENABLED=           # comma list of services/ml-service/models.toml names to load; empty = the entries marked enabled
# ML_EMBED_MAX_ITEMS=256       # /v1/embeddings: more inputs than this is a 400, never truncated
# ML_EMBED_MAX_CHARS=8000      # /v1/embeddings: a longer input is a 400
# ML_EMBED_BATCH_SIZE=32       # texts per model pass
# ML_CLASSIFY_MAX_CHARS=4000   # /v1/decide: a longer `state` is a 413
# ML_CLASSIFY_DEADLINE_MS=600  # /v1/decide: a model slower than this hands the request to the fallback (it stays ready); the deadline is per model
# LABELSET_DIR=/app/labelsets  # where label sets (<name>.json, answered by POST /v1/decide) are read; mount your own (services/ml-service/labelsets/README.md)
# ML_MODEL_RETRY_BASE_S=5      # a model that failed to load is retried after this, doubling...
# ML_MODEL_RETRY_MAX_S=300     # ...up to this cap

# --- Skills and subagents (patterns 45, 46) ---
# DOMAINS_DIR=app/domains      # <domain|shared>/{skills,subagents}/ under it are the catalogs; relative to the working directory, the image ships it
# SKILLS_COLLECTION=skills     # separate Qdrant collection for skill_search (`make index-skills`)
# SKILLS_SEARCH_TOP_K=1        # 1 on purpose: with qwen2.5:3b even one distractor made it hedge
# SUBAGENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=45  # wall-clock cap on ONE nested subagent run

# --- Tenancy and memory ---
# DEFAULT_TENANT=ecorp       # the tenant scripts/tools/seed.py stamps on sample documents
# MEMORY_RETENTION_DAYS=365  # an older memory is invisible at recall time

# --- Semantic cache (pattern 22) ---
# SEMANTIC_CACHE_REDIS_URL=                 # where the answer cache lives; empty = REDIS_URL. Production gives it its own `redis-cache` (a cache wants allkeys-lru; the queue, locks and revocations must never be evicted)
# SEMANTIC_CACHE_SIMILARITY_THRESHOLD=0.95  # cosine; a query must be nearly identical in meaning
# SEMANTIC_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# SEMANTIC_CACHE_ENABLED=true               # false: neither read nor written. Only a conversation's first question uses it; read agent_semantic_cache_total by outcome and turn it off if hits are a small share

# --- Context and answers ---
# FOLLOWUPS_ENABLED=true              # false: no domain suggests follow-ups after a cited answer (one more model call per cited turn); a domain's own switch is `suggest_followups` on its manifest
# STALE_TOOL_RESULT_MAX_CHARS=2000    # an EARLIER turn's tool result longer than this reaches the model as a short note (the stored conversation keeps it whole); 0 = off
# CONTEXT_GAP_REASK_ENABLED=true      # an answer that opens by saying the pre-fetched documents lack it is asked again without them, so a tool can be used; the first answer returns if none is (pattern 105). One extra model call on those turns
# OUTPUT_CHECKS_OFF_ON_ROUTED_TIERS=  # comma-separated output-check codes skipped on a turn routed to medium or strong (e.g. too_short,uncited; see agent_output_check_fired_total{check,tier}); leaked_prompt and fabricated are refused; empty = all on

# --- Queue and workers (patterns 43, 44; WORKER_CONCURRENCY.md) ---
# The domains one agent-worker process serves: comma-separated names, or `all` (what the containers use). `make agent-worker-all` sets it.
AGENT_WORKER_DOMAINS=ecorp
# AGENT_WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENCY=10            # concurrent turns per agent-worker process
# INGEST_WORKER_MAX_CONCURRENCY=10           # concurrent ingest jobs per ingest-worker process
# WORKER_RECLAIM_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60         # how often each worker polls for entries a dead worker abandoned
# AGENT_WORKER_RECLAIM_IDLE_SECONDS=300      # unacked this long = presumed dead. Must exceed REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS x (1 + UNATTENDED_MAX_DECLINE_ROUNDS) and the conversation lock's 60 s, each by 30 s, or the app refuses to start
# INGEST_WORKER_RECLAIM_IDLE_SECONDS=900     # ingest has no wall-clock timeout, so this is generous
# REQUESTS_STREAM_MAX_AGE_SECONDS=86400      # a reclaim pass drops requests-stream entries older than this (the net under "ack then delete"); must exceed both reclaim idles; floor 3600
# MAX_AUTO_RECLAIM_RETRIES=1                 # re-publishes of one reclaimed job before it is dead-lettered
# CHAT_SUBMIT_DEDUP_TTL_SECONDS=10           # an identical resubmission inside this window reuses the first attempt
# CHAT_FIRST_RESPONSE_DEADLINE_SECONDS=30    # how long a queued chat job may show no event and have no worker holding its conversation; not a limit on the turn
# SSE_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS=15                   # a chat stream silent this long gets an SSE comment (`: keepalive`) so a proxy does not close it; keep below the shortest idle timeout in front of the API
# INGEST_FIRST_RESPONSE_DEADLINE_SECONDS=30  # same, for ingest jobs

# --- Spend ceilings ---
# Local Ollama models carry a small SHADOW price in deploy/dev/litellm-config.yaml (not a bill: it lets the meter and these caps see local usage), so the ceilings stay far away
# until OPENAI_API_BASE is a paid provider.
# MAX_TOKENS_PER_TURN=40000               # cumulative tokens in one turn before the next tool call is refused; each call re-sends the prompt and tool schemas
# MAX_COST_USD_PER_TURN=0.5               # hard stop before the next tool/LLM call in one turn
# MAX_SUBAGENT_COST_USD_PER_RUN=0.15      # the only enforcement on one nested subagent call
# MAX_COST_USD_PER_TENANT_PER_DAY=20.0    # rolling 24h, against the usage events (one row per model call)
# MAX_COST_USD_PER_TENANT_PER_MONTH=0     # calendar month (UTC), resets on the 1st; 0 = no cap
# MAX_COST_USD_PER_PRINCIPAL_PER_DAY=0    # one person's rolling 24h inside their tenant; 0 = no cap
# MAX_COST_USD_PER_PRINCIPAL_PER_MONTH=0  # one person's calendar month; 0 = no cap
# PRICING_REFRESH_SECONDS=3600            # how long a model price read from LiteLLM /model/info is trusted
# UNPRICED_MODEL_POLICY=allow             # allow | block: block refuses turns (and subagent runs) on a model LiteLLM cannot price
# BUDGET_POLICY_REFRESH_SECONDS=30        # how long per-tenant/per-person limit overrides are cached per process; 0 = off
# BUDGET_CHECK_FAILURE_POLICY=open        # open | closed: what the tenant allowance does when it cannot read the spend
# USAGE_EVENTS_ENABLED=true               # per-call usage events (postgres-init/19-usage-events.sql); the caps sum them, so `false` is REFUSED at startup: leave it unset

# --- Time ceilings ---
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60  # outermost wall-clock bound on one turn
# LLM_READ_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=   # longest wait for the gateway to send ANYTHING (between stream chunks, not the answer's length); unset = REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS; 30 suits a hosted model; never above REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# LLM_SDK_MAX_RETRIES=0       # retries the OpenAI SDK makes inside one call; 0, because the graph retries three times and the gateway twice already (3 x 3 x 3 = 27 provider calls otherwise)

# --- Retention and sweeps ---
# USAGE_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=400          # `make usage-events-sweep` deletes older usage events; floor 35 (the caps and the reconciliation read them); the wallet is never touched
# BILLING_USAGE_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=400  # how long `make billing-usage-sweep` keeps the billing package's usage events (floor 35); an event whose export never finished is kept
# BILLING_INBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=400        # how long `make billing-inbox-sweep` keeps applied/ignored webhook rows (floor 30)
# CHECKPOINT_RETENTION_DAYS=0             # `make checkpoint-sweep` deletes a conversation (checkpoints and session row) this many days after its last message; 0 = off, nothing is deleted until you choose. Usage events, the wallet and memories are untouched
# ANSWER_SAMPLE_SIZE=50                   # `make answer-sample` reads this many of yesterday's conversations and counts invented source numbers and missing or wrong attributions; counts only, no text; advisory
# ANSWER_SAMPLE_TENANTS=                  # comma-separated; empty = every tenant (the job reads answers across tenants: leave it unscheduled if your data policy forbids that)

# --- Credits and billing ---
# CREDITS_PER_USD=                           # credits per US dollar of model cost. NO default: a price is your decision. Unset = credits off for the whole deployment (nothing rated, debited, gated or shown); 1000 makes 1 credit = $0.001. Apply postgres-init/21-usage-event-credits.sql BEFORE setting it; at most 6 decimals
# MARKUP=1                                   # multiplier on cost when pricing a call in credits (1 = at cost, 1.5 = cost + 50%); only with CREDITS_PER_USD
# CREDITS_ENFORCEMENT=false                  # true refuses a turn (insufficient_credits) for a tenant whose available credits, less in-flight holds, are not positive. Needs CREDITS_PER_USD; false with a rate set still debits wallets ("shadow mode")
# CREDIT_CHECK_FAILURE_POLICY=open           # open | closed: what the credit gate does when it cannot read the wallet (only when enforcing); open serves and alerts CreditGateUnenforced
# BILLING_PROVIDERS=                         # comma-separated payment providers whose webhooks POST /billing/webhooks/{name} accepts (empty = none); each needs a secret below or the process refuses to start. Set it the SAME in the API, the agent workers and the export worker. `fake` is the dev/test adapter: never in production
# BILLING_WEBHOOK_SECRETS={}                 # JSON {"provider": "signing secret"}; keep it in .env, never in the repo
# BILLING_WEBHOOK_MAX_BODY_BYTES=262144      # a larger webhook body is refused (413) before it is read
# BILLING_WEBHOOK_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=600  # per source address, on the webhook route only
# BILLING_WEBHOOK_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5             # failed applies of one event before it is quarantined (alert BillingWebhookQuarantined) instead of retried forever
# BILLING_REFUND_HOLD_HOURS=24               # how long a refund that arrives before its purchase is held (provider retries) before it is quarantined
# BILLING_EXPORT_BATCH_SIZE=100              # usage events sent to a provider per worker pass (`make billing-export-worker`)
# BILLING_EXPORT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30         # how often the export worker looks for due events
# BILLING_EXPORT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=10             # retryable failures of one event before it is given up on (alert UsageExportFailed)
# BILLING_EXPORT_BACKOFF_BASE_SECONDS=30     # after the k-th failed attempt an event waits min(base * 2**(k-1), cap)
# BILLING_EXPORT_BACKOFF_CAP_SECONDS=21600   # the longest a retry ever waits (6 hours)
# BILLING_EXPORT_MAX_AGE_DAYS=30             # an event still unsent after this is expired, counted and alerted (max 34: Stripe rejects events older than 35 days)
# BILLING_EXPORT_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30     # the deadline for one provider call (the worker holds the batch's locks while it waits)

# --- Payment providers: Stripe and Polar credentials (specs/010 T029) ---
# The STRIPE and POLAR adapters are built: put `stripe` and/or `polar` in BILLING_PROVIDERS (each with its signing secret in BILLING_WEBHOOK_SECRETS, as {"stripe": "whsec_...",
# "polar": "..."}) and set its API credential below to let it create checkouts. A PAYPAL adapter is NOT BUILT: an unregistered name in BILLING_PROVIDERS makes the API refuse to
# start. Use SANDBOX credentials, and keep every value in .env, never in the repo.
# STRIPE_API_KEY=            # a server-side key from the sandbox's API keys page; restricted (rk_test_) is recommended over secret (sk_test_); a publishable key (pk_) is refused. Blank = not set (webhooks are still verified; a checkout is refused)
# POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=        # an Organization Access Token from the Polar SANDBOX organization (a production token does not work there). Blank = not set (webhooks are still verified; a checkout is refused)
# POLAR_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox  # sandbox | production (sandbox-api.polar.sh or api.polar.sh/v1); keep sandbox until you are ready to take real money

# --- Reconciliation (`make usage-reconcile`, `make credit-reconcile` and their workers) ---
# USAGE_RECONCILE_TOLERANCE_USD=0.01       # the meter against the LLM gateway's spend log: a per-tenant-per-day difference is drift only above the LARGER of this and the percentage below
# USAGE_RECONCILE_TOLERANCE_PCT=1          # percent of the larger of the two figures compared (absorbs rounding differences on a busy tenant)
# USAGE_RECONCILE_SETTLE_SECONDS=900       # leave the newest traffic out of a run: the gateway writes its spend log in batches. Keep above REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS plus the gateway's batch interval
# CREDIT_RECONCILE_SETTLE_SECONDS=900      # the same for the wallet check (was every recorded usage charged?)
# USAGE_RECONCILE_LOOKBACK_DAYS=2          # whole UTC days each gateway run re-reads (1-35); the gateway is asked for every spend-log row in the window
# CREDIT_RECONCILE_LOOKBACK_DAYS=2         # whole UTC days the wallet check re-reads (1-35)
# USAGE_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=21600   # how often `make usage-reconcile-worker` runs a pass
# CREDIT_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=21600  # how often `make credit-reconcile-worker` runs a pass
# USAGE_RECONCILE_GATEWAY_MAX_PAGES=200    # ceiling on gateway pages (1000 rows each) per run; past it the comparison is reported INCOMPLETE, never as a short sum

# =====================================================================
# Part 3: developer tooling (evaluation judges and security scanners)
# =====================================================================

# --- Google AI Studio key: the dev gateway's two upper tiers, red-team attack generation and the deepeval judge ---
# The LiteLLM container reads it for `chat-medium` and `chat-strong` (deploy/dev/litellm-config.yaml), which nothing calls while ROUTER_MODE=off.
# `make promptfoo-redteam` (and its nightly run, .github/workflows/llm-evals.yml) and two of the three deepeval files (`make deepeval`). The TARGET stays local and offline; only attack
# generation and grading (promptfoo/redteam.yaml's redteam.provider) and the deepeval judge (DEEPEVAL_JUDGE_MODEL, tests/deepeval/conftest.py) use the hosted model.
# Key from https://aistudio.google.com/app/apikey. The free tier's 30 req/min is already paced by promptfoo-redteam's --max-concurrency and --delay.
GOOGLE_API_KEY=

# --- Groq: the conversation-simulator deepeval file only (tests/deepeval/conftest.py's deepeval_conversation_judge) ---
# That file's `KnowledgeRetentionMetric` asks for a Dict-typed structured-output schema that Gemini's response_schema rejects (400 INVALID_ARGUMENT on "additional_properties"), so it stays
# on Groq's `openai/gpt-oss-120b` for both roles (simulating the customer and grading). Key from https://console.groq.com/keys. The account's real limits, read off the x-ratelimit-*
# headers: ~8,000 TPM and 1,000 requests per DAY (not per minute), refilling continuously.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# --- Judge failover (tests/deepeval/fallback.py, `make deepeval`) ---
# Each judge answers through a chain: its primary, other models on the same provider (same key, own free quota), then the optional backup providers below. A hand-over happens only on a
# rate limit, an overload or a timeout. Both lists are comma-separated; `none` turns one off; blank means the default shown. Not secrets, so CI repo VARIABLES (llm-evals.yml's deepeval job).
# DEEPEVAL_JUDGE_FALLBACK_MODELS=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# DEEPEVAL_CONVERSATION_JUDGE_FALLBACK_MODELS=openai/gpt-oss-20b,qwen/qwen3.8-27b

# --- Backup judges: Plugsky, then OpenRouter (the last links of the chain; tests/deepeval/backup_providers.py) ---
# Optional, free, OpenAI-compatible. Each is enabled by its own key alone, and both fixtures behave as before with neither. Tried after the primary and the same-provider models are rate
# limited or overloaded; a non-transient error (bad key, retired model id) stops the chain and fails loudly.
# 1) Plugsky: `plugsky-micro` (Nemotron 3 Super 120B), then `plugsky-lite`. Key at https://plugsky.com/dashboard. Its free plan's 30 req/min is documented per plan, not per model, so the
#    second model only helps if that turns out to be per model, or when the first one's upstream is what is overloaded.
PLUGSKY_API_KEY=
# Ordered model ids; blank = `plugsky-micro,plugsky-lite`; `none` switches Plugsky off even with a key (the knob's original name, kept for existing .env files).
# DEEPEVAL_BACKUP_MODEL=plugsky-micro,plugsky-lite
# 2) OpenRouter: `nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free`, then `google/gemma-4-31b-it:free`. Tried last: `:free` models are limited ACCOUNT-WIDE (20 req/min, 50 requests/day, 1,000/day
#    once the account has bought $10 of credits), so a second model adds a different upstream, not more quota, and one `make deepeval` run can use the 50. Key at
#    https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# Ordered model ids; blank = the two above; `none` switches OpenRouter off. `:free` ids churn (some carry an expiration_date) and a retired one fails as a non-transient 404: re-pick from
# https://openrouter.ai/models?variant=free. Keep it a large model (small ones grade unreliably); `openrouter/free` works but makes scores irreproducible.
# DEEPEVAL_OPENROUTER_MODEL=nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free,google/gemma-4-31b-it:free

# --- DefectDojo (`make defectdojo-up`, `make defectdojo-import`, scripts/tools/defectdojo_import.py; README, "Security scanning & load testing") ---
# Needed only to push a scan report into a running instance (also zap.yml's optional import step). DEFECTDOJO_URL defaults to `make defectdojo-up`'s own port. Generate the key in the
# DefectDojo UI (My Account -> API v2 Key) after logging in with the admin password `make defectdojo-up` prints on first run.
DEFECTDOJO_URL=http://localhost:8080
DEFECTDOJO_API_KEY=

# --- Strix (`make strix`, `make strix-app`; the strix.yml workflow reads repo secrets of the same names) ---
# An autonomous pentest agent outside this app that needs a CLOUD model, not the local Ollama. The Makefile loads exactly these five names from here (not the rest of this file) and unsets
# OPENAI_API_BASE and its aliases for the run, or Strix would send the scan to the local LiteLLM. The model is in LiteLLM's format; a Google AI Studio key (GOOGLE_API_KEY above) goes in
# LLM_API_KEY and needs the `gemini/` prefix, since a bare name is routed to Vertex AI. Leave LLM_API_BASE unset for a hosted provider. EXA_API_KEY and PERPLEXITY_API_KEY are optional
# (web search for the agent; one is enough). Values are read literally, not by a shell: optional matching quotes, no `$VAR`, no trailing `# comment`.
# STRIX_LLM=gemini/gemini-3.6-flash
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_API_BASE=
# EXA_API_KEY=
# PERPLEXITY_API_KEY=
